# OAuth2: Device Code Flow
# For devices without a browser or for testing
# Shows a verification URL and user code; user authorizes on a different device

@baseUrl = https://api.example.com
@oauth2-cache = .tokens.json

###

### Device Code Flow (polling)
# When you run this request:
# 1. A notification shows a verification URL and user code
# 2. You open that URL on another device (phone, computer)
# 3. Enter the user code and authorize
# 4. The client polls the token endpoint until approved
# 5. Token is cached and injected as: Authorization: Bearer <token>

# @oauth2-grant = device_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-device-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/device/code
# @oauth2-client-id = {{CLIENT_ID}}
# @oauth2-scope = read
GET {{baseUrl}}/resource

###

### Device Code for broader scopes
# Multiple scopes separated by space

# @oauth2-grant = device_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-device-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/device/code
# @oauth2-client-id = my-device-client
# @oauth2-scope = read write offline_access
GET {{baseUrl}}/protected

###

### POST with Device Code
# @oauth2-grant = device_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-device-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/device/code
# @oauth2-client-id = {{CLIENT_ID}}
# @oauth2-scope = read write
POST {{baseUrl}}/config
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "setting": "value"
}

###

### Using environment variables
# @oauth2-grant = device_code
# @oauth2-token-url = {{$env OAUTH2_TOKEN_URL}}
# @oauth2-device-url = {{$env OAUTH2_DEVICE_URL}}
# @oauth2-client-id = {{$env OAUTH2_CLIENT_ID}}
# @oauth2-scope = read
GET {{baseUrl}}/data

###

### Token reuse
# Tokens are cached with:
# - Cache key: grant::tokenURL::clientID::scope
# - Subsequent requests with the same parameters reuse the cached token
# - No user action needed on second request (unless token expired)

# First request - shows notification with code
# @oauth2-grant = device_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-device-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/device/code
# @oauth2-client-id = my-client
# @oauth2-scope = openid profile
GET {{baseUrl}}/data/1

###

# Second request - uses cached token (no notification)
# @oauth2-grant = device_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-device-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/device/code
# @oauth2-client-id = my-client
# @oauth2-scope = openid profile
GET {{baseUrl}}/data/2

###

### Use case: CLI tools, embedded devices, or testing
# Device Code Flow is useful when:
# - The device can't open a browser (embedded system, CLI tool)
# - User prefers to authorize on a different device
# - You're testing and don't want browser popups
# - Multi-device setups where one device is headless

### Notification behavior (VS Code extension)
# A notification popup shows:
#   "Authorization required for example.com"
#   "URL: https://auth.example.com/device?code=ABC123"
#   "Enter user code: ABC123"
#   [Open Browser] [Dismiss]
#
# Polling continues automatically in the background
# Token is injected once authorized by user on the other device
